               "adr-2607279200-kotoba-clojure-shaped-safety-elaboration-migration"]
 :adr/body "## Context\n\nThe owner brought an external design analysis (Unison x Lisp x IPLD) proposing that Kotoba adopt a pure S-expression canonical syntax, keep the Clojure-shaped surface separately, hash canonical checked representation rather than raw source, use de Bruijn indices so alpha-equivalent terms share an identity, hash mutually recursive definitions as an SCC, express the IR as an IPLD Schema encoded in DAG-CBOR, and give every parenthesised node a CID while keeping logical identity separate from physical block boundaries.\n\nTwo questions came with it. Can amu, given Kotoba plus IPLD Schema, compile wholly different languages — JS, Rust, C, Java? And can other grammars sit in kototama / osaho / amu in parallel with the pure-S Kotoba grammar?\n\nThe first thing measured was what already exists, because most of the proposal does.\n\n## What the proposal asks for, and where it already stands\n\n| proposal | state |\n|---|---|\n| pure S core: lam, app, ref, perform, handle, rel, query | LANDED 2026-09-06. All seven heads admitted by lang/guest-grammar.edn :sugar :pure-s-expression-core, lowered by kotoba-sema, backends recorded per head |\n| Clojure-shaped surface kept separately | LANDED. .cljk is that surface; .kotoba is the canonical text format. ADR-kotoba-pure-s-expression-core-and-cljk-surface, accepted 2026-09-04 |\n| hash canonical checked repr, not raw source | LANDED. DefCID payload v2 seals typed KIR, profile version, desugar contract version, effect row, interface, direct definition dependency CIDs |\n| names out of the identity (de Bruijn) | LANDED and measured — ADR-2609077000 property 2, and again below |\n| mutual recursion hashed as a unit | LANDED as scc-v1, with member ordering taken from canonical bytes rather than names |\n| IPLD Schema as the semantic model, DAG-CBOR as canonical bytes, CID as identity | DECIDED 2026-09-07, ADR-2609076000. io-ipld owns codec/schema/ADL; the KIR schema itself is not written yet |\n| logical identity separate from physical block boundary | DECIDED in two places. ADR-2609076000 decision 5 keeps schema and representation version OUT of the identity payload; packed KIR representations are ADLs. The storage half is ADR-2608160100 (block -> CARv2 pack -> object) |\n| explicit (call f a) rather than implicit head-application | LANDED as app, and measured to cost nothing: (app (ref inc1) n) and (inc1 n) produce identical hir-sha256, kir-sha256 and wasm32 bytes |\n\n**Where Kotoba is already past the proposal.** The seal is not only the term. It also carries the EFFECT ROW and the INTERFACE including the transitive schema closure, which is why widening a record moves the CID of every function that takes one even though no body changed. Unison does not do that, and the proposal does not ask for it.\n\n## What the proposal is right about and is not built\n\n**Per-node identity.** Kotoba's granularity is the top-level definition. There is no CID for a subexpression, and the proposal's own caveat is the correct one: a subterm under a binder is an OPEN TERM, and content-addressing it naively is unsound because the same textual subtree means different things under different binder stacks. Kotoba avoids that today by only ever sealing closed definitions.\n\nThe cheap version of what the proposal actually wants — an agent that edits by saying replace this node with that node instead of regenerating a file — does not need a second identity. It needs a PATH into an already-sealed block, and io-ipld already has bounded traversal producing CAR/GraphSync proof blocks (ipld.selector, ipld.graph). Building a parallel per-node CID space would be a second identity for the same meaning, which decision 5 of ADR-2609076000 exists to prevent.\n\n## What the proposal is wrong about for Kotoba\n\n**Removing vector, map and set literals.** Rejected. The stated reason — that {:a 1} and (hash-map :a 1) let one meaning diverge into two forms — is already answered one layer down: both are :sugar, both desugar to the same primitives, and the identity never sees a literal. Removing them buys nothing at the identity layer and costs the pretraining prior that .cljk exists to exploit. The dual-surface decision already assigned this axis correctly.\n\n## The surface-convergence claim, measured 2026-09-08\n\nADR-kotoba-pure-s-expression-core-and-cljk-surface section 3 says both surfaces mint identical Definition CIDs for equivalent normalized semantics. Its 2026-09-06 addendum records that as NOT delivered, and names a beta-redex probe as the concrete first gap, citing DIFFERENT hir-sha256 and kir-sha256.\n\n**Those are not the definition CID.** The claim is about DefCIDs; the evidence was two intermediate text hashes. Measured on amu origin/main 473e84ab through bin/amu definition-cids --jvm-free, three independent surface pairs — including the exact pair the addendum names — mint IDENTICAL CIDs:\n\n| pair | pure spelling | clojure-shaped spelling | run CID |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A (the addendum's probe) | (app (ref inc1) (app (lam [x] (+ x 1)) n)) | (inc1 (let [g (fn [x] (+ x 1))] (g n))) | bafyreifbyisa5f... |\n| B (threading) | (app (ref inc1) (app (ref inc1) n)) | (-> n inc1 inc1) | bafyreicjnack62... |\n| C (cond) | (if (< n 0) 0 n) | (cond (< n 0) 0 :else n) | bafyreic5fnjazk... |\n\nFor pair A the agreement is over ALL FOUR definitions in the module, synthetic lambda-lift names included, not only the export. The negative control moves: changing (+ x 1) to (+ x 2) gives bafyreicn3hzig6..., so identical is discriminating rather than degenerate.\n\n**So the recorded gap was an artifact of measuring the wrong hash, and section 3 holds on every pair measured.** Two limits are stated rather than glossed. First, three pairs are not a corpus: destructuring, protocol dispatch, match and the document forms are unmeasured, and this ADR does not claim them. Second, the addendum's own kir-sha256 finding was NOT re-measured — amu check --json does not emit that field — so both halves stand together: the intermediate hashes differ AND the identities agree, which is exactly what a desugaring frontend with a name-free identity should produce.\n\n**The measurement was also nearly lost to a stale checkout.** Run first against the shared orgs/kotoba-lang/amu, the pure spelling returned :subset-reject, which reads as the language refusing the form. It was the checkout: 156 commits behind origin/main, pinning a kotoba-sema older than the heads. Reported as a language limit, that would have entered the record as a gap that does not exist.\n\n## Decision\n\n**1. A grammar is a surface. It may not add authority.** The acceptance bar for any new grammar is the one the pure-S core already met and recorded: no new runtime representation, no new capability, no new head at any backend. A surface that needs a new primitive to exist is not a surface, it is a language change, and it goes through lang/guest-grammar.edn as one.\n\n**2. The registration point already exists and is kotoba-core-contracts resources/kotoba/lang/source_contract.edn.** :source-kinds keys an extension to a :reader-target and a :safe-gate-required? flag. Today every kind reads EDN. A non-Lisp grammar is a new :reader-target, not a new compiler.\n\n**3. osaho and kototama are grammar-agnostic and stay that way.** osaho owns checked IR and DefCID and has no notion of source text; kototama states it does not own the language. In amu's eleven-stage elaboration pipeline exactly ONE stage is text-aware — :closed-reader, whose rules are :closed-edn-subset and :fail-closed-on-unknown-reader-macro. Everything from :name-module-resolution onward already operates on core forms. That is the seam, and it is already clean.\n\n**4. Compiling JS / Rust / C / Java as source frontends is mechanically possible and is NOT what would result.** kotoba-hir validates a closed module/function envelope without repeating source-language type inference, which is the contract of a multi-frontend compiler. But Kotoba's safety is not in the IR, it is in what the frontend REFUSES: 26 forbidden heads, deny-by-default capabilities, interprocedural effect inference as a fixpoint, no ambient authority, and untracked control effect banned permanently. Run those refusals against each language and what survives is not the language:\n\n- JS loses eval, with, prototype mutation, ambient globals and dynamic host-object access\n- C loses pointers, which ARE ambient authority over memory, against a KIR admission gate that takes one-word typed values\n- Rust fits best semantically — Result maps to [:result T E], traits to protocols, no ambient exceptions — and least usefully, because the whole point is that amu does not want a second borrow checker\n- Java loses classloading, reflection, and exceptions, and exceptions as an untracked control effect are one of exactly two things this language names as PERMANENTLY forbidden\n\nSo the deliverable would be **X-shaped Kotoba**, exactly as .cljk is Clojure-shaped Kotoba and not Clojure. Name any such surface that way from the first commit. A surface that calls itself JS will be read as a promise that JS programs compile, and they will not.\n\n**5. Foreign languages as RUNTIME INPUT is the supported route today, and it is proven.** kotoba-lang/org-ecma-international-262 is a JavaScript interpreter written in Kotoba and compiled by amu, passing 283/283 of its own tests inside wasm32 under the real browser host. JavaScript source is a string the compiled program reads; amu never sees it. This route needs no new grammar, no new reader target, and no new refusal set, and it generalises to any language by writing its interpreter in Kotoba. **Reach for this first.**\n\n**6. The gate on a third surface is a convergence CORPUS, not a probe.** Section 3 is discharged for three pairs, which is enough to retire the recorded gap and not enough to license a third grammar. Before a new surface is registered in source_contract.edn it brings a conformance corpus that pairs each of its constructs with a canonical-core spelling and asserts equal DefCIDs, in the shape lang/code-identity-conformance already uses, with a negative control in it. Three surfaces without that corpus is three unverified claims, not one verified language.\n\n**7. A grammar that does not share the desugar contract does not share identities.** :desugar-contract-version is one of the six sealed DefCID inputs. A frontend that lowers differently seals a different number, so the same program in two grammars mints two identities. Either a new surface desugars into the existing core forms under the existing contract version, or it declares its own and its divergence is stated up front rather than discovered.\n\n## What is NOT claimed\n\n- Section 3 is not proven in general. Three pairs converged; destructuring, protocol dispatch, match and document forms were not measured.\n- The addendum's kir-sha256 result was not reproduced, only reinterpreted. amu check --json does not carry that field.\n- No non-Lisp frontend was prototyped. This decides the seam and the naming rule, not a schedule.\n- Per-node addressing is not decided here, only its hazard and the cheaper mechanism that already exists.\n- Nothing here is a gate. No check enforces that a registered :source-kind desugars into the admitted core-form set, and this ADR does not invent one.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nThe shared checkout runs 156 behind and cannot answer this; use a worktree at origin/main.\n\n    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/amu worktree add --detach /tmp/amu-main origin/main\n    node /tmp/amu-main/bin/amu definition-cids <file> --jvm-free\n\nWrite the same function in both spellings, compare the run CID lines — not hir-sha256 and not kir-sha256, which is the substitution this ADR corrects — and include the negative control, because a comparison that only ever reports agreement cannot be told from one that never ran."}]
